\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Why does shape remain approximately stationary?}
The softmax architecture and sampling hyperparameters that determine distributional shape are fixed across depths; recursive generation shifts context toward high-probability regions but does not alter the generation mechanism itself.
This predicts approximate stationarity under standard decoding, confirmed by sampling ablation ($t \in [0.5, 0.7]$: ${\leq}0.3$\,pp degradation; $t = 1.0$: $-6$\,pp; Appendix~\ref{app:sampling-sensitivity}), and implies the phenomenon reflects a tendency of standard decoding rather than an architectural guarantee.
Entropy std ($|\rho| = 0.17$) is a borderline case between the drifting central-tendency features ($|\rho| > 0.60$) and the near-stationary tail decay ($|\rho| = 0.04$), consistent with a feature that partially captures location-dependent variance.

\paragraph{Prompt-type dependence.}
Semantic-preservation constraints attenuate drift (elaboration F1\,=\,0.730 vs.\ continuation 0.781; Appendix~\ref{app:prompt-type}), consistent with \citet{broken_telephone}: the more a task anchors the output distribution to the input, the less room recursive drift has to accumulate.
Cross-type transfer remains viable (F1\,=\,0.753).

\paragraph{Practical applicability.}
Document-level gen-1 per-class F1\,=\,0.63 is insufficient for automated filtering but sufficient for corpus-level flagging, where error scales as $O(1/\sqrt{n})$.
Misclassifications concentrate in shallow gen-2+ samples where drift has not yet accumulated (Appendix~\ref{app:error-analysis}), consistent with DDR predictions.

\paragraph{The gen-1\,/\,gen-2+ boundary.}
The primary classification difficulty is distinguishing gen-1 from gen-2+: 86\% of binary errors are false negatives (gen-2+ predicted as gen-1), with depth-2 error rate at 48\% compared to 20\% for depth-4.
This asymmetry follows directly from DDR: the first recursive step produces the smallest location shift, so depth-2 text occupies a region of feature space that overlaps heavily with gen-1.
Larger generators exacerbate the overlap (Mistral-7B error rate 45\% vs.\ Pythia-1B 18\%), consistent with higher-capacity models producing text that more closely matches the statistical properties of human-sourced input.

\paragraph{Relation to model collapse.}
Model collapse \cite{curse_of_recursion,strong_model_collapse} describes degradation when models are \emph{retrained} on their own outputs, progressively losing distributional coverage through parameter updates.
DDR operates in a fundamentally different regime: inference-chain drift occurs with fixed parameters, and is fully reversible by changing the seed text.
The two phenomena also diverge in their distributional signatures: model collapse predicts progressive tail shrinkage as minority modes are lost from the training distribution \cite{curse_of_recursion}, whereas DDR preserves distributional shape (entropy max, tail decay remain stationary) while shifting only the location.
This suggests that inference-chain drift reflects compression \emph{within} the model's existing capacity rather than \emph{erosion} of that capacity, and may represent a pre-collapse regime where distributional narrowing is detectable but not yet irreversible.
Topic drift shows weak correlation with depth features ($|\rho| < 0.29$; embedding similarity between successive generations correlates with entropy drift at $\rho = -0.286$), confirming it mediates rather than confounds depth signal.

\paragraph{Granularity and robustness.}
Three classes represent the practical resolution ceiling: 5-class LOGO F1 drops to $\approx$0.50, with depths 2--4 mutually indistinguishable (Appendix~\ref{app:finer-depth}).
That said, QLoRA fine-tuning recovers 3-class F1\,=\,0.905 (Appendix~\ref{app:qlora}), confirming that richer signal exists beyond the seven summary statistics, though at the cost of interpretability and the DDR decomposition that is a core contribution of this work.
Under text perturbation, LLM-simulated sentence regeneration (50\% edit distance) causes comparable degradation to 5\% random word replacement (Appendix~\ref{app:human-edit}), confirming that depth signal resides in generative structure rather than surface token statistics.

\paragraph{Instruction tuning.}
Instruction-tuned models show substantially weaker fingerprints (OLMo-Instruct LOGO F1\,=\,0.338 vs.\ 0.763 base).
Length regulation and format constraints imposed by alignment compress the distributional variation that depth fingerprinting exploits, justifying our restriction to base models as the appropriate setting for isolating the phenomenon.

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related}

\subsection{AI-Generated Text Detection}
\label{sec:related-detection}

Methods for distinguishing human-written from machine-generated text have evolved from statistical tests \cite{gltr,grover,release_strategies} to zero-shot reference-model approaches \cite{detectgpt,fast_detectgpt,binoculars,dna_gpt} and learned classifiers \cite{ghostbuster,feat_aug_transformer,reliable_detection,ld_score}.
Recent work pursues diverse signals including intrinsic dimensionality \cite{intrinsic_dimension}, surprisal diversity \cite{diveye}, and distributional gap measures \cite{generator_barrier}; see \citet{wu_survey_2025} and \citet{jawahar_survey} for surveys.
A persistent limitation is that these methods produce binary (real vs.\ synthetic) decisions.
Recent multi-class extensions (role recognition in human-LLM collaboration \cite{beyond_binary}, multi-generator attribution \cite{semeval2024_task8}) discriminate among different \emph{sources} rather than recursive generation \emph{depth}.
Even when detection scores correlate with depth, they exhibit high cross-generator variance as ordinal predictors (binary F1 from 0.52 to 0.71; Table~\ref{tab:baselines}), because each captures a single distributional property that generalizes inconsistently \cite{paraphrase_evades,detection_possibilities}.
Adversarial training approaches (e.g., RADAR \cite{radar}) improve robustness to paraphrasing but remain binary detectors without depth awareness.
Our contribution is not a new detection method but the task formulation (ordinal depth estimation, framed within the broader ordinal classification literature \cite{gutierrez_ordinal_survey,coral_ordinal}) and the finding that combining drifting and stationary features yields consistent cross-generator performance.
This is orthogonal to source attribution \cite{uchendu_attribution,munir_looking_glass}, which identifies \emph{which} model generated text; our LOGO evaluation decouples generator identity from depth.

\subsection{Model Collapse and Iterative Generation}
\label{sec:related-collapse}

Self-consuming generative models progressively lose distributional coverage \cite{self_consuming_mad}, and model collapse is inevitable without fresh human data \cite{curse_of_recursion}, though careful data mixing can mitigate this \cite{model_collapse_avoidable,strong_model_collapse}.
These studies focus on \emph{training-loop} degradation; our setting studies \emph{inference chains}, where a fixed model generates text conditioned on its own prior output without retraining.
Iterative self-refinement \cite{self_refine} represents another form of recursive LLM processing, but targets output improvement through feedback loops rather than open-ended continuation; the distributional drift we study arises specifically from unconstrained recursive generation.

Two concurrent works are most directly relevant.
\citet{broken_telephone} analyze paraphrasing and translation chains, showing progressive information loss.
\citet{markovian_chains} demonstrate that semantic-preservation constraints induce attractor dynamics bounding distinguishable depth to approximately three classes; their analysis focuses on characterizing convergence behavior through model-based perplexity tracking.
We differ in three respects: (i) we study \emph{non-paraphrastic continuation} chains, where the absence of semantic anchoring permits richer depth signal; (ii) we use handcrafted features rather than model-internal representations, enabling the DDR decomposition into drifting vs.\ stationary components; and (iii) we focus on \emph{estimating} depth from text rather than characterizing the convergence dynamics of the generation process.

\subsection{Vocabulary Diversity and Stylometry}
\label{sec:related-diversity}

Declining lexical diversity in LLM-generated text is documented in both training \cite{curious_decline} and deployment \cite{writing_diversity} settings.
Our surface features (TTR and hapax ratio) are classical stylometric measures \cite{stamatatos_survey,koppel_authorship} that we apply to inference chains, showing monotonic diversity decline across depths without model retraining.
We push beyond generator identity \cite{uchendu_attribution} to the \emph{depth} dimension, and show that diversity declines monotonically even under top-$p$ sampling \cite{nucleus_sampling}.

\subsection{Data Quality and Provenance}
\label{sec:related-provenance}

Scalable data curation relies on quality signals \cite{dsir,qurating,ccnet}; generation depth is a complementary provenance signal characterizing \emph{how many steps removed} text is from a human source.
Proactive watermarking \cite{watermark_llm,synthid_text,aaronson_watermark} embeds identifiable patterns, but watermark strength decays across multi-step chains \cite{chainwash}, motivating passive post-hoc methods like ours.
At the feature level, our NLL analysis connects to \citet{min_k_percent}, who use the probability tail for membership inference; we analyze the full NLL distribution for ordinal depth estimation.

\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

\subsection{Depth Classification Performance}
\label{sec:results-classification}

The 7-feature ensemble achieves 3-class LOGO F1\,=\,0.762 (95\% CI [0.758, 0.766]; $N$\,=\,37{,}058) across eight generators from five families (Table~\ref{tab:logo-results}).
Because gen-0 seeds are shared across generators (\S\ref{sec:generation}), the 3-class metric may carry upward bias from memorized gen-0 patterns; we therefore also report a leak-free binary metric (gen-1 vs.\ gen-2+): F1\,=\,0.694, CI [0.688, 0.700], which is unaffected by this sharing.
Per-generator binary F1 ranges from 0.633 (Mistral-7B) to 0.724 (Pythia-2.8B); the primary difficulty is distinguishing gen-1 from gen-2+ (Appendix~\ref{app:confusion}).
Larger generators show moderate degradation (Qwen3-8B: 0.692, Mistral-7B: 0.633) relative to the 0.694 average.

\paragraph{Cross-model generalization.}
Cross-model chains (randomly selected generator per step) yield binary F1\,=\,0.764 under 5-fold CV,\footnote{Cross-model chains use the original five-generator subset (Pythia, OLMo, Qwen); Llama and Mistral were added later for single-generator evaluation.} confirming that depth signal persists when text passes through heterogeneous generators.
Leave-one-family-out (LOFO) evaluation yields average binary F1\,=\,0.686 ($\Delta$\,=\,$-$0.8\,pp vs.\ LOGO), confirming transfer across architectural families under the strictest unseen-generator test.
Appendix~\ref{app:domain} further demonstrates that the signal generalizes across text domains, though with substantial variation (Wikipedia binary F1\,=\,0.789 vs.\ Academic 0.628 at $t{=}0.7$; all domains $>$\,0.73 at $t{=}1.0$).

\paragraph{Length confound control.}
Length-matched subsampling yields binary F1\,=\,0.724 (vs.\ 0.770 unmatched; $\Delta$\,=\,$-$0.046); a random-subsample control at equal $N$ (F1\,=\,0.767) confirms the drop reflects deconfounding, not sample-size reduction.
Length accounts for $\approx$6\% of discriminative performance (Appendix~\ref{app:length}).


