\subsection{Downstream Utility}
\label{sec:results-downstream}

If depth fingerprinting reliably identifies recursive text, can it improve data curation?
Fine-tuning OLMo-2-0425-1B on five data variants (3 seeds each) yields two conclusions.
At equal sample count ($n$\,=\,3{,}951), depth-aware selection is practically equivalent to random subsampling (ppl 2.387 vs.\ 2.367; TOST $\pm$2\% relative margin, $p = 1.1 \times 10^{-4}$).
However, data quantity dominates: the full dataset ($n$\,=\,12{,}299, ppl\,=\,2.335) yields significantly lower perplexity than any depth-filtered subset, and TOST rejects equivalence at the same margin (Appendix~\ref{app:downstream}).
Depth composition per se does not determine training utility at fixed sample count.

\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Task Formulation}
\label{sec:task}

Given a text $x$, the goal is to predict its \emph{generation depth} $d \in \{0, 1, 2{+}\}$, where $d{=}0$ denotes human-written text, $d{=}1$ denotes first-generation LLM output conditioned on human text, and $d{=}2{+}$ denotes text produced by recursively conditioning an LLM on its own prior output at least twice.
Formally, let $\mathcal{C}_0$ be a reference corpus of human-written text.
A generation chain of depth $k$ is defined recursively: $x^{(0)} \sim \mathcal{C}_0$, and $x^{(k)} = G(x^{(k-1)})$ for $k \geq 1$, where $G$ denotes stochastic LLM generation conditioned on its input.
We frame depth estimation as an ordinal classification task over three classes rather than binary real-vs-synthetic detection, enabling finer-grained provenance characterization.
Depths beyond 2 are merged into a single $2{+}$ class, consistent with monotonic feature convergence that compresses inter-class margins beyond depth 2 (\S\ref{sec:results-differential}).

\subsection{Generation Chain Construction}
\label{sec:generation}

We construct generation chains using eight generators spanning five model families: \textbf{Pythia} (2.8B, 1B) \cite{pythia}, \textbf{OLMo} (OLMo-2-1B) \cite{olmo}, \textbf{Qwen} (Qwen3-1.7B, Qwen3-0.6B, Qwen3-8B-Base) \cite{qwen3}, \textbf{Llama} (Llama-3.2-3B) \cite{llama3}, and \textbf{Mistral} (Mistral-7B-v0.3) \cite{mistral7b}.
This selection covers parameter scales from 0.6B to 8B and diverse pretraining corpora, enabling evaluation of cross-generator and cross-scale generalization.

For each generator, we sample approximately 1{,}000 seed passages from English Wikipedia as $\mathcal{C}_0$.
The same pool of seed passages serves as gen-0 for all generators.
We use shared seeds across generators to ensure that cross-generator comparisons control for input content, isolating the effect of the generation model from source text variation.
In LOGO evaluation, gen-0 samples from the held-out generator's chains also appear (with the same text) in the training generators' chains, creating a potential train--test overlap for the gen-0 class.
We address this with a supplementary binary evaluation excluding gen-0 entirely (\S\ref{sec:results-baselines}).
Each seed is iteratively fed through the generator to produce a chain of depth 4 (i.e., gen-0 through gen-4), using the prompt ``\texttt{Continue the following text: \{text\}}''.
All generation uses temperature $\tau{=}0.7$, nucleus sampling with $p{=}0.9$, and maximum length of 256 new tokens.
The theoretical yield is $8 \times {\sim}1{,}000 \times 5 \approx 40{,}000$ texts; after removing generation failures, exact duplicates, and passages shorter than 50 tokens, 37{,}058 texts remain (Appendix~\ref{app:dataset-stats}).
For classification, depths 2--4 are collapsed into the $2{+}$ class.

\subsection{Feature Extraction}
\label{sec:features}

The seven features are motivated by two complementary views of distributional drift.
Reference-model features capture how the text's token-level surprise distribution shifts relative to a fixed model; surface features capture how lexical diversity contracts under recursive generation.
Together, they span both the generative mechanism (what the model produces) and the surface manifestation (how the vocabulary changes).
This two-pronged design is not post-hoc: it follows directly from the hypothesis that recursive generation induces a location shift in the NLL distribution (\S\ref{sec:results-differential}).

We extract seven distributional features per text, organized into two groups based on what they measure rather than how they are computed.

\paragraph{Reference-model features.}
A held-out reference model (Pythia-410M, chosen to be smaller than all generators) computes per-token negative log-likelihood (NLL) for each text.
Although Pythia-410M shares the GPT-NeoX architecture with two of the eight generators, cross-family reference model experiments (\S\ref{sec:results-differential}) confirm that depth features are not reference-specific (LOGO F1 varies by $\leq$0.013 across Pythia, OLMo, and Qwen references).
From the resulting NLL sequence, we extract five statistics:
\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
    \item \textbf{entropy\_mean}\footnote{We use ``entropy'' as shorthand for token-level negative log-likelihood under the reference model, following convention in prior detection work. These are point-wise NLL values, not Shannon entropy of the full distribution.}: mean token-level NLL, capturing the average predictability of the text under the reference model;
    \item \textbf{entropy\_std}: standard deviation of token-level NLL, measuring the spread of per-token surprise;
    \item \textbf{entropy\_max}: maximum token-level NLL, capturing the most surprising token;
    \item \textbf{entropy\_min}: minimum token-level NLL, capturing the most predictable token. As generation depth increases, the floor of token-level surprise drops, reflecting the model's increasing confidence on its own outputs.
    Although entropy\_min shows near-zero marginal impact in leave-one-feature-out analysis ($\Delta$F1\,=\,+0.01\,pp), we retain it for completeness of the NLL summary statistics (mean, std, max, min) and because removing it does not improve performance;
    \item \textbf{tail\_decay}: slope of the vocabulary-level averaged log-probability distribution in the mid-rank region (ranks 100--1000).
    For each text, we compute the mean log-probability of each vocabulary token across all positions under the reference model, sort tokens in descending order of mean log-probability, and fit a linear regression to ranks 100--1000.
    The slope captures how steeply the reference model's probability mass decays beyond high-probability tokens, characterizing the shape of the distribution tail.
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Surface features.}
Two features capture lexical diversity without requiring a reference model:
\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
    \item \textbf{TTR}: type-token ratio (number of unique word types divided by total tokens);
    \item \textbf{hapax\_ratio}: proportion of words appearing exactly once (\emph{hapax legomena}).
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Feature complementarity.}
The grouping reflects an empirical pattern we term \emph{differential depth response}: central-tendency and diversity features (entropy\_mean, TTR, hapax\_ratio) show substantial monotonic drift with generation depth ($|\rho| = 0.60$--$0.63$); entropy\_min and entropy\_std show moderate drift ($|\rho| = 0.17$--$0.41$); and shape features (entropy\_max, tail\_decay) remain near-stationary ($|\rho| < 0.10$).
This differential behavior, consistent with recursive generation shifting the location of the NLL distribution while preserving distributional shape, motivates combining both feature types.
As shown in \S\ref{sec:results}, the full 7-feature set significantly outperforms either group alone.

\subsection{Evaluation Protocol}
\label{sec:evaluation}

\paragraph{Cross-validation.}
To evaluate generalization to unseen generators, we use \textbf{Leave-One-Generator-Out (LOGO)} cross-validation: in each fold, one of the eight generators is held out for testing and the remaining seven provide training data.
We additionally report \textbf{Leave-One-Family-Out (LOFO)} results, where an entire model family (e.g., all Qwen models) is held out, testing generalization across architectural lineages.

\paragraph{Classifier.}
We use XGBoost \cite{xgboost} as the classifier throughout.
Gradient-boosted trees handle heterogeneous feature scales and nonlinear interactions without extensive hyperparameter tuning, and their feature importance scores support interpretability.
All methods in Table~\ref{tab:baselines} use identical XGBoost hyperparameters ($n\_\text{estimators}{=}200$, $\max\_\text{depth}{=}6$, learning rate $0.1$) for controlled comparison.
LOGO F1 is stable across random seeds (std\,$<$\,0.001 over 5 seeds), confirming that results are not sensitive to classifier initialization.
Alternative classifiers (random forest, logistic regression) yield comparable performance (Appendix~\ref{app:classifier}), confirming that discriminative power resides in the features, not the classifier.
We evaluated two ordinal-specific methods (CORAL \cite{coral_ordinal}, Frank-Hall ordinal ensemble \cite{frank_hall_ordinal}); neither significantly outperforms multiclass XGBoost (CIs overlapping; \S\ref{sec:results}), confirming that with only three ordinal levels, ordinal structure provides minimal additional benefit.

\paragraph{Metrics and statistical testing.}
The primary metric is \textbf{macro-averaged F1} over the three depth classes, which weights minority classes (gen-1, gen-2+) equally against the typically easier gen-0 class.
We report per-class F1 scores and 95\% confidence intervals via \emph{percentile bootstrap} ($n{=}1{,}000$), resampling at the document level within each held-out fold.
For pairwise model comparisons (e.g., full features vs.\ ablated subsets), we use the \textbf{McNemar test} on disagreement tables.
For comparisons against detection baselines, we use \textbf{paired bootstrap} significance tests.
All reported $p$-values are two-sided.
We report four pairwise comparisons (ensemble vs.\ each individual baseline) in \S\ref{sec:results-baselines}; applying Bonferroni correction ($\alpha_{\text{adj}} = 0.05/4 = 0.0125$), all remain significant at $p < 0.001$.
Feature--depth correlations (\S\ref{sec:results-differential}) are computed on class-balanced subsamples (equal samples per depth class) to control for depth-class imbalance in the generation pipeline.

\paragraph{Baselines.}
We compare the 7-feature ensemble against four single-score detection methods repurposed as ordinal depth predictors: Avg-LogProb, FastDetectGPT \cite{fast_detectgpt}, Binoculars \cite{binoculars}, and LD-Score \cite{ld_score}.
Each baseline score is used as a univariate feature in the same XGBoost framework under LOGO cross-validation, ensuring a controlled comparison.

\paragraph{Length confound control.}
Since TTR and hapax\_ratio are mechanically length-dependent, we verify that depth signal is not an artifact of systematic length variation.
We conduct three complementary analyses: (1) length--depth correlation to verify absence of systematic length variation, (2) length--feature correlation to identify features with moderate length dependence, and (3) classification on length-matched subsets that equalize length distributions across depth classes (\S\ref{sec:results-classification}).

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Why does shape remain approximately stationary?}
The softmax architecture and sampling hyperparameters that determine distributional shape are fixed across depths; recursive generation shifts context toward high-probability regions but does not alter the generation mechanism itself.
This predicts approximate stationarity under standard decoding, confirmed by sampling ablation ($t \in [0.5, 0.7]$: ${\leq}0.3$\,pp degradation; $t = 1.0$: $-6$\,pp; Appendix~\ref{app:sampling-sensitivity}), and implies the phenomenon reflects a tendency of standard decoding rather than an architectural guarantee.
Entropy std ($|\rho| = 0.17$) is a borderline case between the drifting central-tendency features ($|\rho| > 0.60$) and the near-stationary tail decay ($|\rho| = 0.04$), consistent with a feature that partially captures location-dependent variance.

\paragraph{Prompt-type dependence.}
Semantic-preservation constraints attenuate drift (elaboration F1\,=\,0.730 vs.\ continuation 0.781; Appendix~\ref{app:prompt-type}), consistent with \citet{broken_telephone}: the more a task anchors the output distribution to the input, the less room recursive drift has to accumulate.
Cross-type transfer remains viable (F1\,=\,0.753).

\paragraph{Practical applicability.}
Document-level gen-1 per-class F1\,=\,0.63 is insufficient for automated filtering but sufficient for corpus-level flagging, where error scales as $O(1/\sqrt{n})$.
Misclassifications concentrate in shallow gen-2+ samples where drift has not yet accumulated (Appendix~\ref{app:error-analysis}), consistent with DDR predictions.

\paragraph{The gen-1\,/\,gen-2+ boundary.}
The primary classification difficulty is distinguishing gen-1 from gen-2+: 86\% of binary errors are false negatives (gen-2+ predicted as gen-1), with depth-2 error rate at 48\% compared to 20\% for depth-4.
This asymmetry follows directly from DDR: the first recursive step produces the smallest location shift, so depth-2 text occupies a region of feature space that overlaps heavily with gen-1.
Larger generators exacerbate the overlap (Mistral-7B error rate 45\% vs.\ Pythia-1B 18\%), consistent with higher-capacity models producing text that more closely matches the statistical properties of human-sourced input.

\paragraph{Relation to model collapse.}
Model collapse \cite{curse_of_recursion,strong_model_collapse} describes degradation when models are \emph{retrained} on their own outputs, progressively losing distributional coverage through parameter updates.
DDR operates in a fundamentally different regime: inference-chain drift occurs with fixed parameters, and is fully reversible by changing the seed text.
The two phenomena also diverge in their distributional signatures: model collapse predicts progressive tail shrinkage as minority modes are lost from the training distribution \cite{curse_of_recursion}, whereas DDR preserves distributional shape (entropy max, tail decay remain stationary) while shifting only the location.
This suggests that inference-chain drift reflects compression \emph{within} the model's existing capacity rather than \emph{erosion} of that capacity, and may represent a pre-collapse regime where distributional narrowing is detectable but not yet irreversible.
Topic drift shows weak correlation with depth features ($|\rho| < 0.29$; embedding similarity between successive generations correlates with entropy drift at $\rho = -0.286$), confirming it mediates rather than confounds depth signal.

\paragraph{Granularity and robustness.}
Three classes represent the practical resolution ceiling: 5-class LOGO F1 drops to $\approx$0.50, with depths 2--4 mutually indistinguishable (Appendix~\ref{app:finer-depth}).
That said, QLoRA fine-tuning recovers 3-class F1\,=\,0.905 (Appendix~\ref{app:qlora}), confirming that richer signal exists beyond the seven summary statistics, though at the cost of interpretability and the DDR decomposition that is a core contribution of this work.
Under text perturbation, LLM-simulated sentence regeneration (50\% edit distance) causes comparable degradation to 5\% random word replacement (Appendix~\ref{app:human-edit}), confirming that depth signal resides in generative structure rather than surface token statistics.

\paragraph{Instruction tuning.}
Instruction-tuned models show substantially weaker fingerprints (OLMo-Instruct LOGO F1\,=\,0.338 vs.\ 0.763 base).
Length regulation and format constraints imposed by alignment compress the distributional variation that depth fingerprinting exploits, justifying our restriction to base models as the appropriate setting for isolating the phenomenon.

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related}

\subsection{AI-Generated Text Detection}
\label{sec:related-detection}

Methods for distinguishing human-written from machine-generated text have evolved from statistical tests \cite{gltr,grover,release_strategies} to zero-shot reference-model approaches \cite{detectgpt,fast_detectgpt,binoculars,dna_gpt} and learned classifiers \cite{ghostbuster,feat_aug_transformer,reliable_detection,ld_score}.
Recent work pursues diverse signals including intrinsic dimensionality \cite{intrinsic_dimension}, surprisal diversity \cite{diveye}, and distributional gap measures \cite{generator_barrier}; see \citet{wu_survey_2025} and \citet{jawahar_survey} for surveys.
A persistent limitation is that these methods produce binary (real vs.\ synthetic) decisions.
Recent multi-class extensions (role recognition in human-LLM collaboration \cite{beyond_binary}, multi-generator attribution \cite{semeval2024_task8}) discriminate among different \emph{sources} rather than recursive generation \emph{depth}.
Even when detection scores correlate with depth, they exhibit high cross-generator variance as ordinal predictors (binary F1 from 0.52 to 0.71; Table~\ref{tab:baselines}), because each captures a single distributional property that generalizes inconsistently \cite{paraphrase_evades,detection_possibilities}.
Adversarial training approaches (e.g., RADAR \cite{radar}) improve robustness to paraphrasing but remain binary detectors without depth awareness.
Our contribution is not a new detection method but the task formulation (ordinal depth estimation, framed within the broader ordinal classification literature \cite{gutierrez_ordinal_survey,coral_ordinal}) and the finding that combining drifting and stationary features yields consistent cross-generator performance.
This is orthogonal to source attribution \cite{uchendu_attribution,munir_looking_glass}, which identifies \emph{which} model generated text; our LOGO evaluation decouples generator identity from depth.

\subsection{Model Collapse and Iterative Generation}
\label{sec:related-collapse}

Self-consuming generative models progressively lose distributional coverage \cite{self_consuming_mad}, and model collapse is inevitable without fresh human data \cite{curse_of_recursion}, though careful data mixing can mitigate this \cite{model_collapse_avoidable,strong_model_collapse}.
These studies focus on \emph{training-loop} degradation; our setting studies \emph{inference chains}, where a fixed model generates text conditioned on its own prior output without retraining.
Iterative self-refinement \cite{self_refine} represents another form of recursive LLM processing, but targets output improvement through feedback loops rather than open-ended continuation; the distributional drift we study arises specifically from unconstrained recursive generation.

Two concurrent works are most directly relevant.
\citet{broken_telephone} analyze paraphrasing and translation chains, showing progressive information loss.
\citet{markovian_chains} demonstrate that semantic-preservation constraints induce attractor dynamics bounding distinguishable depth to approximately three classes; their analysis focuses on characterizing convergence behavior through model-based perplexity tracking.
We differ in three respects: (i) we study \emph{non-paraphrastic continuation} chains, where the absence of semantic anchoring permits richer depth signal; (ii) we use handcrafted features rather than model-internal representations, enabling the DDR decomposition into drifting vs.\ stationary components; and (iii) we focus on \emph{estimating} depth from text rather than characterizing the convergence dynamics of the generation process.

\subsection{Vocabulary Diversity and Stylometry}
\label{sec:related-diversity}

Declining lexical diversity in LLM-generated text is documented in both training \cite{curious_decline} and deployment \cite{writing_diversity} settings.
Our surface features (TTR and hapax ratio) are classical stylometric measures \cite{stamatatos_survey,koppel_authorship} that we apply to inference chains, showing monotonic diversity decline across depths without model retraining.
We push beyond generator identity \cite{uchendu_attribution} to the \emph{depth} dimension, and show that diversity declines monotonically even under top-$p$ sampling \cite{nucleus_sampling}.

\subsection{Data Quality and Provenance}
\label{sec:related-provenance}

Scalable data curation relies on quality signals \cite{dsir,qurating,ccnet}; generation depth is a complementary provenance signal characterizing \emph{how many steps removed} text is from a human source.
Proactive watermarking \cite{watermark_llm,synthid_text,aaronson_watermark} embeds identifiable patterns, but watermark strength decays across multi-step chains \cite{chainwash}, motivating passive post-hoc methods like ours.
At the feature level, our NLL analysis connects to \citet{min_k_percent}, who use the probability tail for membership inference; we analyze the full NLL distribution for ordinal depth estimation.

